The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (pru->data->is_k3 && is_iram) {
    ret = pru_rproc_memcpy(ptr, elf_data + phdr->p_offset, filesz);
}
```
Reason:
The condition `if (pru->data->is_k3 && is_iram)` restricts the use of the custom `pru_rproc_memcpy` function to only when the memory segment is in IRAM. This limitation causes unaligned access issues when attempting to load firmware segments into Data RAM, leading to kernel crashes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (pru->data->is_k3) {
    ret = pru_rproc_memcpy(ptr, elf_data + phdr->p_offset, filesz);
}
```
Reason:
By removing the `is_iram` check, the custom `pru_rproc_memcpy` function is now used for all memory segments on K3 SoCs, including Data RAM. This change ensures that firmware loading is handled correctly, preventing unaligned access issues and thus avoiding kernel crashes.

